---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import { href, LINKS, STORE, tracked } from '../data/site';
import TeacherMock from '../components/TeacherMock.astro';

const url = new URL(Astro.url.pathname, Astro.site).toString();
const jsonLd = {
  '@context': 'https://schema.org',
  '@type': 'Article',
  headline: 'Archive the Canvas courses you teach',
  description:
    "What a teacher's copy holds, how students' work is left out unless you include it, Canvas's own course package, and a note for IT and privacy staff.",
  datePublished: '2026-10-24',
  dateModified: '2026-10-24',
  inLanguage: 'en',
  mainEntityOfPage: url,
  publisher: { '@id': new URL('/#org', Astro.site).toString() },
};
---

<Base
  title="Archive a Canvas course you teach – Canvas Course Downloader"
  description="Save the courses you teach before a term closes or your school changes systems: pages, files, assignments and quizzes, with students' work only if you include it."
  ogTitle="Archive the Canvas courses you teach"
  ogType="article"
  jsonLd={jsonLd}
  current="/for-teachers/"
>
  <section class="wrap narrow page-head">
    <h1>Archive the Canvas courses you teach</h1>
    <p class="lead">
      Save a course before the term closes or your school moves to another system. Everything you
      made stays readable on your own computer, and students' work stays out unless you choose it.
    </p>
    <p style="margin-top: 24px">
      <a class="btn btn-primary btn-lg" href={tracked(STORE.chrome, 'teachers')} data-install data-fallback={href('/download/')}>Add to Chrome, it's free</a>
    </p>
  </section>

  <div class="wrap narrow not-print" style="margin-bottom: 56px">
    <div style="max-width: 760px"><TeacherMock /></div>
  </div>

  <article class="wrap narrow prose">
    <h2>What your copy holds</h2>
    <p>The extension sees that you teach the course and saves what you made:</p>
    <ul>
      <li>Files in their folders, pages, assignments with their descriptions and rubrics, and announcements.</li>
      <li>Quizzes with their questions.</li>
      <li>Discussions with their prompts.</li>
      <li>The syllabus, the modules in order, and a calendar file with every deadline (<code>Deadlines.ics</code>).</li>
      <li>An overview page, <code>Open me</code>, that opens in any browser without internet.</li>
    </ul>

    <h2>Students' work stays out unless you include it</h2>
    <p>
      A copy you might share or keep for years shouldn't hold student data by accident. So a
      teacher's copy leaves out the roster, submissions, the gradebook, quiz attempts and named
      discussion replies, and its receipt says <strong>Student data: none included</strong>.
    </p>
    <p>
      Turn on <strong>Include students' work</strong> in the settings when you do need it, for
      example for an archive your school requires. Then the copy also holds every student's
      submission with all attempts, the gradebook and roster, quiz results, rubric scores and
      comments.
    </p>

    <h2>Canvas's own course package</h2>
    <p>
      Optionally, your copy can bring the <code>.imscc</code> course package Canvas builds for
      importing a course again, saved next to the readable copy. It's off by default. Canvas makes
      this package on its servers, lists it under the course's Settings › Export Course Content,
      where your co-teachers can see it, and keeps it there for 30 days.
    </p>
    <p>
      The package is a Common Cartridge file, a standard format. Moodle, Brightspace and Blackboard
      can import Common Cartridge too, though how much of a course comes across differs per system,
      so try one course before moving many.
    </p>

    <h2>Many courses at once</h2>
    <p>
      On your Canvas dashboard, <strong>Download courses</strong> lists current and past courses
      by term. Tick as many as you like; they save one after the other in their own tab, each with
      its own receipt.
    </p>

    <h2 id="it">A note for IT and privacy staff</h2>
    <p class="muted">You can print this page or save it as a PDF to forward it.</p>
    <ul>
      <li>No server: the extension runs in the browser and talks only to your Canvas, with the teacher's own login. No API token, no account.</li>
      <li>Nothing is sent to the developer. No analytics or tracking in the extension.</li>
      <li>Files are saved to the computer's Downloads folder, or a folder the user picks.</li>
      <li>It writes nothing to Canvas, except the course package above, which only a teacher can start.</li>
      <li>The code is open source under the MIT license, and the <a href={href('/privacy/')}>privacy policy</a> lists every permission and why it's needed.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>
      Questions? <a href={LINKS.issues}>Open an issue on GitHub</a>; it's read by the person who
      wrote the code.
    </p>
  </article>
</Base>
